✅ Primitive Data Types
Java has 8 primitive types: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean.
They store simple values like numbers, characters, or true/false.
Size is fixed for each type.
Stored in stack memory.

✅ Primitive Types and Their Wrapper Classes
int → primitive type for integers.
Integer → non-primitive wrapper class for int.
long → primitive type for long integers.
Long → wrapper class for long.
float → primitive type for floating-point numbers.
Float → wrapper class for float.
Other examples:
double → primitive, Double → wrapper.
char → primitive, Character → wrapper.
boolean → primitive, Boolean → wrapper.

✅ Why wrappers exist?
Wrappers allow primitives to be used as objects (needed for collections, generics, etc.).
Auto Boxing: The process of converting primitive to wrapper class is called autoboxing
Auto UnBoxing:The process of converting wrapper class to primitive is called auto unboxing.

✅ Non-Primitive Data Types
Non-primitive types include String, arrays, classes, and javainterfaces.
They store references to objects, not actual values.
Size is not fixed; depends on the object.
Stored in heap memory.

What is a Java Variable?
A variable provides us with named storage that our programs can manipulate.
Each variable in Java has a specific type, which determines the size and layout of the variable's memory;
the range of values that can be stored within that memory;
the set of operations that can be applied to the variable.

Java Variables Types:
•	Local Variables
•	Class Variables (Static Variables)
•	Instance Variables (Non-static Variables)

✅ Local Variables
Declared inside a method, constructor, or block.
Scope is limited to that method or block.
Must be initialized before use (no default value).
Stored in the stack memory.

✅ Instance (Non-Static) Variables
Declared inside a class but outside any method, without static.
Each object gets its own copy.
Have default values (e.g., 0 for int, null for objects).
Stored in the heap as part of the object.

✅ Static Variables
Declared inside a class with the static keyword.
Shared by all objects of the class.
Have default values.
Stored in the method area (class-level memory).

Java Identifiers:
All Java components require names. Names used for classes, variables, and methods are called identifiers. In Java, there are several points to remember about identifiers. They are as follows −
•	All identifiers should begin with a letter (A to Z or a to z), currency character ($) or an underscore (_).
•	After the first character, identifiers can have any combination of characters.
•	A keyword cannot be used as an identifier.
•	Most importantly, identifiers are case sensitive.
•	Examples of legal identifiers: age, $salary, _value, __1_value.
•	Examples of illegal identifiers: 123abc, -salary.

Java Keywords
The following list shows the reserved words in Java.
These reserved words may not be used as constant or variable or any other identifier names.
✅ Data Types
boolean, byte, char, short, int, long, float, double

✅ Access Modifiers
public, private, protected

✅ Class/Interface/Inheritance
class, interface, enum, extends, implements

✅ Object & Memory
new, this, super

✅ Control Flow
if, else, switch, case, default
do, while, for, break, continue, return

✅ Exception Handling
try, catch, finally, throw, throws

✅ Packages & Imports
package, import

✅ Modifiers
abstract, final, static, synchronized, transient, volatile

✅ Others
void, null, true, false

✅ Class Loading & Reflection
instanceof

✅ Advanced Features
assert, native, strictfp

✅ Newer Keywords
const, goto (reserved but not used)